1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,494
When a light moves in a game,

2
00:00:01,494 --> 00:00:05,262
why can brightness remain on a wall while a
shadow follows the change?

3
00:00:05,262 --> 00:00:09,090
We will connect DOOM’s sector brightness to
Quake’s lightmaps,

4
00:00:09,090 --> 00:00:13,272
real-time shadows, and the ordering of forward
and deferred shading.

5
00:00:13,272 --> 00:00:18,598
You will be able to distinguish stored data
from per-frame work and identify

6
00:00:18,598 --> 00:00:23,840
where an image’s limitations come from. Let us
begin with a single surface.

7
00:00:24,560 --> 00:00:29,220
Making a pixel starts with identifying the
visible surface.

8
00:00:29,220 --> 00:00:35,342
The next task is estimating how much light
that surface sends toward the camera.

9
00:00:35,342 --> 00:00:40,590
Rasterization projects triangles onto the
screen and finds the covered samples.

10
00:00:40,590 --> 00:00:44,772
A depth test selects the visible surface when
triangles overlap.

11
00:00:44,772 --> 00:00:50,355
Visibility alone does not calculate lighting.
Position, surface normal, material,

12
00:00:50,355 --> 00:00:56,402
and light data are needed to determine color.
The normal describes a surface’s orientation.

13
00:00:56,402 --> 00:01:02,160
For a worked example, the dot product of an
upward unit normal and an upward light

14
00:01:02,160 --> 00:01:07,832
direction is one. At sixty degrees it is one
half. In this simple diffuse model,

15
00:01:07,832 --> 00:01:12,042
with other factors unchanged, the angular term
is halved.

16
00:01:12,042 --> 00:01:16,838
Normalizing both vectors keeps their lengths
from changing a comparison intended

17
00:01:16,838 --> 00:01:21,633
to measure orientation. Visibility is a
separate factor: even a perfectly aligned

18
00:01:21,633 --> 00:01:26,222
surface receives no direct contribution from
that light if the path is blocked.

19
00:01:26,222 --> 00:01:30,685
The rendering equation adds emitted light to
reflected incoming light.

20
00:01:30,685 --> 00:01:35,512
Material response and the cosine of the
incident angle contribute to that sum.

21
00:01:35,512 --> 00:01:42,291
Rather than deriving the entire integral, we
will follow how games divide this work.

22
00:01:42,291 --> 00:01:48,592
The directions they omit and the values they
store affect the resulting image.

23
00:01:48,592 --> 00:01:52,860
Rays can perform different tasks: finding a
visible surface,

24
00:01:52,860 --> 00:01:58,523
testing visibility to a light, or finding a
surface along a reflection direction.

25
00:01:58,523 --> 00:02:03,472
A rasterized image can still use ray-based
calculations. Rays may compute

26
00:02:03,472 --> 00:02:08,422
lighting before gameplay, while the running
game reads the stored result.

27
00:02:08,422 --> 00:02:11,844
Our first choice replaces complex light
transport with

28
00:02:11,844 --> 00:02:15,120
a brightness value assigned to each part of
the world.

29
00:02:15,840 --> 00:02:20,924
DOOM’s basic 1993 lighting starts with a
sector brightness value.

30
00:02:20,924 --> 00:02:27,552
The map assigns brightness to a sector, and
the renderer uses it to choose a color table.

31
00:02:27,552 --> 00:02:30,938
The source uses a bit shift of sector
brightness to derive a light-table index,

32
00:02:30,938 --> 00:02:32,118
with extra lighting added.

33
00:02:32,118 --> 00:02:35,092
It then applies wall-orientation adjustments
and clamps the index.

34
00:02:35,092 --> 00:02:40,387
Horizontal and vertical walls receive
different adjustments. This is an

35
00:02:40,387 --> 00:02:46,022
axis-based rule, rather than a continuous
calculation of the angle to a light.

36
00:02:46,022 --> 00:02:50,742
Two perpendicular walls can appear at
different brightness in the same sector. The

37
00:02:50,742 --> 00:02:55,799
sector has not changed; the wall orientation
changes the color-table selection. This is

38
00:02:55,799 --> 00:03:00,922
a simple orientation distinction rather than
continuous lighting over a curved surface.

39
00:03:00,922 --> 00:03:07,025
Distance-related behavior also enters through
scale-dependent color-table selection.

40
00:03:07,025 --> 00:03:11,622
Reducing the process to a single sector lookup
would omit that stage.

41
00:03:11,622 --> 00:03:16,507
This is not a per-light visibility test
between an object and a lamp. The scheme alone

42
00:03:16,507 --> 00:03:21,322
does not automatically produce a local light
footprint or a precise object shadow.

43
00:03:21,322 --> 00:03:23,849
The footage is labeled as the 2024 rerelease.

44
00:03:23,849 --> 00:03:28,769
Later lighting added by a modern source port
is not evidence of the 1993 implementation,

45
00:03:28,769 --> 00:03:32,824
even on the same DOOM map. We inspect the
original sector-brightness and

46
00:03:32,824 --> 00:03:36,880
wall-orientation rules separately in released
source and our diagram.

47
00:03:37,600 --> 00:03:42,033
Quake stores more detailed lighting on
surfaces. We distinguish the texture

48
00:03:42,033 --> 00:03:47,012
containing the wall’s appearance from the
lightmap containing lighting on that wall.

49
00:03:47,012 --> 00:03:50,155
Surface samples test visibility to lights.
Orientation, distance,

50
00:03:50,155 --> 00:03:53,352
and light strength contribute to values
calculated before gameplay.

51
00:03:53,352 --> 00:03:57,462
Our diagram fills samples in sequence and
transfers the results to a texture.

52
00:03:57,462 --> 00:04:00,812
Samples behind the box are darker because the
light is blocked.

53
00:04:00,812 --> 00:04:05,952
A wall texture may contain fine detail while
its lightmap uses coarse samples.

54
00:04:05,952 --> 00:04:11,947
Raising texture resolution alone does not
refine the lighting boundary. Raising lightmap

55
00:04:11,947 --> 00:04:17,942
resolution improves stored lighting detail
without inventing detail in the wall texture.

56
00:04:17,942 --> 00:04:21,447
A later engine demonstration makes the
distinction observable.

57
00:04:21,447 --> 00:04:25,716
The 2017 Unreal comparison rooms are labeled
with lightmap resolutions of 128,

58
00:04:25,716 --> 00:04:29,603
256, 512, and 1,024. Separate texture detail
from lighting boundaries.

59
00:04:29,603 --> 00:04:34,572
The camera visits different authored rooms;
this is not a single-object on/off experiment.

60
00:04:34,572 --> 00:04:38,525
At runtime, the renderer combines stored
lighting with the surface texture.

61
00:04:38,525 --> 00:04:40,958
Their coordinates and resolutions can differ;

62
00:04:40,958 --> 00:04:43,572
texture detail and lightmap detail need not
match.

63
00:04:43,572 --> 00:04:47,663
Quake II’s 1997 tools add reflected-light
patch contributions to the final lightmap

64
00:04:47,663 --> 00:04:51,582
samples, allowing baked indirect light to
contribute beyond directly lit areas.

65
00:04:51,582 --> 00:04:56,925
This should not be labeled photon mapping.
Algorithms can share the goal of

66
00:04:56,925 --> 00:05:02,352
storing indirect light while calculating it
through different processes.

67
00:05:02,352 --> 00:05:07,798
Thinking in separate layers explains update
cost. Changing a wall texture and moving

68
00:05:07,798 --> 00:05:13,244
a light are different edits. The latter
changes which samples receive illumination,

69
00:05:13,244 --> 00:05:18,462
so stored visibility and indirect lighting
must be checked against the new scene.

70
00:05:18,462 --> 00:05:22,722
Moving a light exposes the limitation: stored
values describe the old configuration.

71
00:05:22,722 --> 00:05:25,464
New visibility and orientation require
recalculation

72
00:05:25,464 --> 00:05:27,622
or an additional dynamic-lighting method.

73
00:05:27,622 --> 00:05:30,852
A moving character is not attached to one wall
location.

74
00:05:30,852 --> 00:05:34,562
Quake includes a downward query that samples
surface lighting.

75
00:05:34,562 --> 00:05:39,440
Static surface storage and a moving object’s
lighting query are separate concerns.

76
00:05:40,160 --> 00:05:46,338
Texture lookup creates another problem. A
distant screen pixel can cover many texels;

77
00:05:46,338 --> 00:05:51,162
sampling only one can make the pattern
unstable as the camera moves.

78
00:05:51,162 --> 00:05:56,178
Mipmaps store progressively smaller versions
of a texture. Selecting an appropriate

79
00:05:56,178 --> 00:06:00,713
level avoids repeatedly resolving details
finer than the pixel’s footprint.

80
00:06:00,713 --> 00:06:05,425
Bilinear filtering weights four neighboring
texels within one level.

81
00:06:05,425 --> 00:06:09,903
Our diagram changes their weights as the
sample moves between them.

82
00:06:09,903 --> 00:06:14,287
Take a horizontal fraction of one quarter and
a vertical fraction of one half.

83
00:06:14,287 --> 00:06:19,143
The top-left and bottom-left weights are each
three eighths; the right-hand weights

84
00:06:19,143 --> 00:06:23,662
are each one eighth. They sum to one, and the
nearer left values contribute more.

85
00:06:23,662 --> 00:06:27,865
Trilinear filtering also blends between two
adjacent mip levels.

86
00:06:27,865 --> 00:06:33,343
It smooths level transitions; it does not
describe lighting along three spatial axes.

87
00:06:33,343 --> 00:06:37,034
At an oblique viewing angle, a pixel’s
footprint becomes elongated.

88
00:06:37,034 --> 00:06:39,961
Anisotropic filtering accounts for this
directional

89
00:06:39,961 --> 00:06:42,953
shape instead of using only a roughly square
footprint.

90
00:06:42,953 --> 00:06:47,724
Filtering does not invent missing texture
detail. It reads existing data more

91
00:06:47,724 --> 00:06:52,640
appropriately for screen samples, with costs
that depend on the chosen quality.

92
00:06:53,360 --> 00:06:57,641
Independent of the lighting model, processing
order matters.

93
00:06:57,641 --> 00:07:04,023
Tile-based rendering divides the screen into
smaller regions and assigns triangles to them.

94
00:07:04,023 --> 00:07:09,025
The 1990s PowerVR lineage illustrates this
hardware approach. We will not project the

95
00:07:09,025 --> 00:07:13,823
complete structure of a modern programmable
renderer onto those earlier devices.

96
00:07:13,823 --> 00:07:19,063
Keeping a tile’s working data close to the
chip can reduce traffic to larger

97
00:07:19,063 --> 00:07:24,803
external buffers. The tile is processed
locally before its result is written out.

98
00:07:24,803 --> 00:07:26,731
In PowerVR’s TBDR terminology,

99
00:07:26,731 --> 00:07:31,659
deferred describes resolving visibility before
shading the required surfaces.

100
00:07:31,659 --> 00:07:36,373
This differs from the G-buffer-based deferred
shading we will discuss later.

101
00:07:36,373 --> 00:07:41,304
The list here contains primitives. Tiled
lighting instead lists lights affecting a

102
00:07:41,304 --> 00:07:46,653
screen region. Similar-looking grids can
contain different data for different purposes.

103
00:07:46,653 --> 00:07:50,225
Skipping hidden opaque shading can help, but
transparency,

104
00:07:50,225 --> 00:07:54,147
discard operations, and intermediate geometry
still have costs.

105
00:07:54,147 --> 00:07:58,000
Other GPUs also reduce work through early
depth tests and caches.

106
00:07:58,720 --> 00:08:04,224
DOOM 3’s 2004 approach calculates direct-light
and shadow responses to moving lights

107
00:08:04,224 --> 00:08:09,503
and objects. This is a different update task
from reading baked indirect lighting.

108
00:08:09,503 --> 00:08:15,072
A simple diffuse model receives more direct
light when its normal faces the light.

109
00:08:15,072 --> 00:08:19,673
That example does not describe every material
or effect in the game.

110
00:08:19,673 --> 00:08:22,857
A normal map stores small-scale surface
orientation in a texture. It can produce

111
00:08:22,857 --> 00:08:26,323
rock-like shading without automatically
changing a flat surface’s outer silhouette.

112
00:08:26,323 --> 00:08:31,779
Official DOOM 3 game footage moves through
dark industrial spaces and shows

113
00:08:31,779 --> 00:08:37,234
illuminated characters and structures. We
first observe that appearance,

114
00:08:37,234 --> 00:08:42,691
then examine shadow construction through
released source and our diagram.

115
00:08:42,691 --> 00:08:46,782
Game pixels alone do not reveal every stencil
operation.

116
00:08:46,782 --> 00:08:51,323
A shadow volume constructs the region behind
an object from boundaries seen by the light.

117
00:08:51,323 --> 00:08:53,866
The released source includes a step that
connects

118
00:08:53,866 --> 00:08:56,892
original and projected boundary points to form
side faces.

119
00:08:56,892 --> 00:09:00,124
Stencil-buffer tests relate that volume to
screen samples

120
00:09:00,124 --> 00:09:03,032
and suppress the corresponding light
contribution.

121
00:09:03,032 --> 00:09:07,750
The method tests a shadowed region rather than
simply placing a dark image on the floor.

122
00:09:07,750 --> 00:09:13,847
The stencil test is not simply painting the
volume sides black. It counts how the test

123
00:09:13,847 --> 00:09:20,030
path crosses front and back faces to determine
whether a sample is inside the shadowed

124
00:09:20,030 --> 00:09:26,470
region. Camera-inside cases also require
careful counting direction and cap handling.

125
00:09:26,470 --> 00:09:29,400
A point-light assumption produces sharp
boundaries,

126
00:09:29,400 --> 00:09:31,821
and large screen coverage can increase cost.

127
00:09:31,821 --> 00:09:36,790
Real-time direct lighting does not by itself
solve indirect reflections or color bleeding.

128
00:09:37,510 --> 00:09:42,362
Half-Life 2 illustrates another 2004 choice:
richer use of baked lighting.

129
00:09:42,362 --> 00:09:48,350
Responding to normal-map orientation requires
more information than one brightness value.

130
00:09:48,350 --> 00:09:52,969
Valve’s contemporary presentation combines
three basis-direction lightmaps

131
00:09:52,969 --> 00:09:57,724
with a surface normal. Our three arrows
represent stored directional components,

132
00:09:57,724 --> 00:09:59,830
rather than three newly added lamps.

133
00:09:59,830 --> 00:10:03,512
The shown code weights each lightmap color by
a saturated

134
00:10:03,512 --> 00:10:07,424
dot product with its basis direction, then
sums the results.

135
00:10:07,424 --> 00:10:11,950
We keep this version separate from variants
that use squared weights.

136
00:10:11,950 --> 00:10:14,879
Suppose the three stored directional colors
differ.

137
00:10:14,879 --> 00:10:18,914
A normal facing the first basis increases that
lightmap’s contribution;

138
00:10:18,914 --> 00:10:23,470
rotating the normal shifts the weights toward
the other directions. Stored colors

139
00:10:23,470 --> 00:10:27,830
stay fixed while their contributions change,
revealing small surface detail.

140
00:10:27,830 --> 00:10:31,905
Changing the normal changes the directional
contributions.

141
00:10:31,905 --> 00:10:37,156
This adds small-scale orientation response
beyond one flat brightness value,

142
00:10:37,156 --> 00:10:41,310
without reconstructing geometry or every
dynamic occlusion.

143
00:10:41,310 --> 00:10:45,670
Our spatial diagram separates a rock-like
surface into actual geometry and

144
00:10:45,670 --> 00:10:50,574
normal-map detail. Changing normal arrows
affects internal shading while the outer

145
00:10:50,574 --> 00:10:55,615
silhouette remains flat. Moving real vertices
also changes silhouette and occlusion.

146
00:10:55,615 --> 00:10:59,430
The distinction is the information each
representation creates.

147
00:10:59,430 --> 00:11:03,165
Moving-object lighting stores and queries data
differently.

148
00:11:03,165 --> 00:11:07,323
It needs spatial samples rather than a
lightmap attached to a surface.

149
00:11:07,323 --> 00:11:12,750
Half-Life 2’s ambient cube is not identical to
every engine’s spherical-harmonic probes.

150
00:11:12,750 --> 00:11:17,671
A later Unreal lighting demonstration displays
spatial samples and moves a character

151
00:11:17,671 --> 00:11:22,456
between a dark room and a bright floor. A
surface-texel grid and samples distributed

152
00:11:22,456 --> 00:11:27,377
through space store information in different
locations. This is a 2017 engine example

153
00:11:27,377 --> 00:11:32,230
of spatial lighting, not the identical
implementation of Half-Life 2 ambient cubes.

154
00:11:32,230 --> 00:11:36,327
Higher-quality baking consumes preparation
time and storage.

155
00:11:36,327 --> 00:11:40,653
Lower runtime cost trades against production
work and update cost.

156
00:11:40,653 --> 00:11:44,750
One map’s bake time cannot serve as a
universal figure for games.

157
00:11:44,750 --> 00:11:49,046
A remaining baked contribution after a light
changes follows from this assumption.

158
00:11:49,046 --> 00:11:52,919
Hybrid systems may update direct light
dynamically while retaining static

159
00:11:52,919 --> 00:11:56,670
indirect light, so the two update schedules
must be examined separately.

160
00:11:57,390 --> 00:12:02,437
A shadow map stores depth from the light. It
records the nearest visible surface,

161
00:12:02,437 --> 00:12:08,003
then transforms a camera-visible point into
the same light coordinates for comparison.

162
00:12:08,003 --> 00:12:13,296
With a depth convention where larger means
farther, a tested point behind the stored

163
00:12:13,296 --> 00:12:18,963
depth is considered blocked. Both values must
use matching coordinates and conventions.

164
00:12:18,963 --> 00:12:24,880
For a numerical example, stored depth of 0.33
and receiver depth of 0.80 place the

165
00:12:24,880 --> 00:12:31,233
receiver behind the first surface. If moving
the blocker changes stored depth to 0.90,

166
00:12:31,233 --> 00:12:35,933
the receiver at 0.80 lies in front and is
unoccluded by this test.

167
00:12:35,933 --> 00:12:41,137
Magnifying the light’s depth image reveals its
texel grid. Covering a large region with

168
00:12:41,137 --> 00:12:46,483
few texels can create stair-stepped shadow
edges, even when the original shape is smooth.

169
00:12:46,483 --> 00:12:51,249
Small numerical differences between stored and
reconstructed depth can cause

170
00:12:51,249 --> 00:12:55,873
a surface to shadow itself. Bias adjusts the
comparison to reduce this error.

171
00:12:55,873 --> 00:12:59,441
Excessive bias can detach the shadow from the
object.

172
00:12:59,441 --> 00:13:04,913
A single chosen value is not guaranteed to
solve every distance and surface slope.

173
00:13:04,913 --> 00:13:10,136
PCF performs several neighboring depth
comparisons and averages their visibility

174
00:13:10,136 --> 00:13:14,993
results. Averaging depths first and comparing
once is a different operation.

175
00:13:14,993 --> 00:13:19,904
If two of four neighboring tests report
visibility, their PCF average is one half.

176
00:13:19,904 --> 00:13:25,100
This produces an intermediate visibility
value, but does not exactly integrate every

177
00:13:25,100 --> 00:13:30,723
path from an area light. Wider filters require
more samples and introduce further tradeoffs.

178
00:13:30,723 --> 00:13:35,891
PCSS searches for blockers and adjusts filter
width using their relation to the receiver.

179
00:13:35,891 --> 00:13:40,447
Variance Shadow Maps instead use depth moments
and have their own limitations,

180
00:13:40,447 --> 00:13:41,943
including light leakage.

181
00:13:41,943 --> 00:13:46,373
Variance Shadow Maps and Unreal’s Virtual
Shadow Maps share an abbreviation but are

182
00:13:46,373 --> 00:13:50,990
different methods. A later episode will
distinguish virtual-page shadow management.

183
00:13:51,710 --> 00:13:56,878
One map covering a broad outdoor scene can
make nearby shadows coarse. Cascaded Shadow

184
00:13:56,878 --> 00:14:01,693
Maps split the camera’s view by distance and
assign shadow maps to those regions.

185
00:14:01,693 --> 00:14:06,132
This does not create more lights. It records
shadows from one directional

186
00:14:06,132 --> 00:14:10,143
light at different spatial densities near and
far from the camera.

187
00:14:10,143 --> 00:14:14,415
For an illustrative example, 2,048 texels
covering 20 meters gives roughly one

188
00:14:14,415 --> 00:14:19,453
centimeter per texel. Covering 200 meters with
the same count gives roughly ten centimeters.

189
00:14:19,453 --> 00:14:22,819
Those numbers are not settings or performance
measurements from a game.

190
00:14:22,819 --> 00:14:26,793
They illustrate how coverage changes
world-space texel size at the same resolution.

191
00:14:26,793 --> 00:14:31,486
The point’s view depth selects a cascade.
Blending near boundaries can soften

192
00:14:31,486 --> 00:14:36,963
transitions, while camera movement may require
stabilization relative to the texel grid.

193
00:14:36,963 --> 00:14:39,056
In the acquired official Unreal example,

194
00:14:39,056 --> 00:14:43,363
the character casts a visible shadow while
running on the floor, but not on the stairs.

195
00:14:43,363 --> 00:14:46,773
The documentation describes disabled CSM
reception on the stairs.

196
00:14:46,773 --> 00:14:49,527
That clip demonstrates receiver configuration,

197
00:14:49,527 --> 00:14:54,774
rather than visualizing cascade partitions. A
separate spatial diagram explains the splits,

198
00:14:54,774 --> 00:14:58,053
while the observed engine result illustrates
the setting.

199
00:14:58,053 --> 00:15:02,779
A 2017 official Unreal demonstration shows the
partitions more directly. Enabling Show,

200
00:15:02,779 --> 00:15:07,816
Advanced, Shadow Frustums displays differently
colored projected boundaries over the scene.

201
00:15:07,816 --> 00:15:12,543
Observe how those boundaries change with the
shadow distance and distribution controls.

202
00:15:12,543 --> 00:15:16,043
The same demonstration changes the cascade
count through four,

203
00:15:16,043 --> 00:15:19,284
zero, one, and three while also adjusting
shadow distance.

204
00:15:19,284 --> 00:15:23,304
Distinguish disabling cascades from assigning
a broad region to one map.

205
00:15:23,304 --> 00:15:28,425
Relate the one-versus-multiple edge comparison
to our equal-resolution coverage diagram.

206
00:15:28,425 --> 00:15:32,963
Several controls change, so we do not
attribute every difference to one parameter.

207
00:15:32,963 --> 00:15:35,768
More cascades require additional maps and
work.

208
00:15:35,768 --> 00:15:40,830
The technique allocates a limited budget while
managing transitions and instability.

209
00:15:41,550 --> 00:15:45,813
Even with shadow information, more lights
increase work.

210
00:15:45,813 --> 00:15:51,990
Forward rendering evaluates material and
relevant lighting as it shades a surface.

211
00:15:51,990 --> 00:15:57,610
Deferred shading first stores visible-surface
depth, normals, and material data

212
00:15:57,610 --> 00:16:03,390
in a G-buffer. The lighting stage reads that
data to accumulate light contributions.

213
00:16:03,390 --> 00:16:07,884
An actual engine can inspect base color,
normals, and roughness separately.

214
00:16:07,884 --> 00:16:12,862
The 2017 Unreal material demonstration
flattens a book model and exports buffer data

215
00:16:12,862 --> 00:16:18,048
as separate images. This connects to distinct
surface information rather than one final

216
00:16:18,048 --> 00:16:23,510
shaded color. Baking materials or exporting
buffers is different from baking illumination.

217
00:16:23,510 --> 00:16:27,415
Suppose two lights contribute 0.25 and 0.35 to
a shaded pixel.

218
00:16:27,415 --> 00:16:32,071
Forward shading adds them while processing the
surface, producing 0.60.

219
00:16:32,071 --> 00:16:37,854
Deferred shading stores surface data first and
accumulates the same contributions later.

220
00:16:37,854 --> 00:16:42,510
The target result can match while intermediate
data and ordering differ.

221
00:16:42,510 --> 00:16:48,003
Four objects and three lights illustrate
ordering, not a universal workload.

222
00:16:48,003 --> 00:16:51,749
Culling can skip pixels outside a light’s
influence,

223
00:16:51,749 --> 00:16:55,910
so not every renderer evaluates every light at
every pixel.

224
00:16:55,910 --> 00:17:02,370
S.T.A.L.K.E.R.’s development materials
describe a deferred approach for many lights.

225
00:17:02,370 --> 00:17:08,830
Its counts and performance figures belong to
the documented scene and hardware context.

226
00:17:08,830 --> 00:17:14,129
The benefit has costs: material data must be
written and read, and one stored

227
00:17:14,129 --> 00:17:19,510
surface does not naturally represent several
translucent layers at a pixel.

228
00:17:19,510 --> 00:17:23,364
Place two glass layers in front of an opaque
wall.

229
00:17:23,364 --> 00:17:29,944
A buffer storing only the nearest surface does
not contain every layer’s material.

230
00:17:29,944 --> 00:17:34,644
Compositing them needs additional information
or passes.

231
00:17:34,644 --> 00:17:40,190
A real engine can therefore combine rendering
paths within one frame.

232
00:17:40,190 --> 00:17:44,494
Hybrid renderers may shade transparency in
separate forward passes.

233
00:17:44,494 --> 00:17:46,901
Light pre-pass is another compromise,

234
00:17:46,901 --> 00:17:51,350
computing lighting from normals and depth
before a later material pass.

235
00:17:51,350 --> 00:17:57,076
Forward rendering remains useful. Unreal’s
Robo Recall example combines feature choices,

236
00:17:57,076 --> 00:18:00,233
multisample anti-aliasing, and VR
requirements.

237
00:18:00,233 --> 00:18:04,270
The relevant question is which work the
implementation reduces.

238
00:18:04,270 --> 00:18:07,547
Missing screen-space features in a particular
Unreal

239
00:18:07,547 --> 00:18:11,109
forward implementation are implementation
constraints,

240
00:18:11,109 --> 00:18:16,310
not proof that such effects are mathematically
impossible in every forward renderer.

241
00:18:16,310 --> 00:18:21,411
We also distinguish this from TBDR’s deferred
terminology. That division concerns visibility

242
00:18:21,411 --> 00:18:26,070
before shading; this division concerns
material storage before lighting evaluation.

243
00:18:26,790 --> 00:18:31,695
Even with shadows, contact regions can appear
flat. Screen-space ambient

244
00:18:31,695 --> 00:18:37,223
occlusion estimates nearby occlusion using
screen depth and neighboring samples.

245
00:18:37,223 --> 00:18:41,057
Nearby obstructions can reduce environmental
illumination.

246
00:18:41,057 --> 00:18:44,964
SSAO approximates this with information
already present on the

247
00:18:44,964 --> 00:18:48,943
screen instead of tracing the complete scene
in every direction.

248
00:18:48,943 --> 00:18:52,142
Our diagram places samples around a box corner
and compares depths.

249
00:18:52,142 --> 00:18:53,826
A small radius emphasizes contact;

250
00:18:53,826 --> 00:18:58,093
a larger one reaches farther but can increase
undersampling and incorrect associations.

251
00:18:58,093 --> 00:19:02,582
Filtering can reduce noise but may blur across
boundaries. Sample count,

252
00:19:02,582 --> 00:19:06,493
filtering, and depth differences must be
considered together.

253
00:19:06,493 --> 00:19:11,385
The depth buffer primarily describes visible
front surfaces. A thin rod in front of a

254
00:19:11,385 --> 00:19:16,345
distant wall does not reveal its thickness
through a single depth value. An excessive

255
00:19:16,345 --> 00:19:21,513
sample range can associate unrelated surfaces
and produce a dark halo around the object.

256
00:19:21,513 --> 00:19:25,654
CryEngine 2’s 2007 technical discussion and
Unreal documentation

257
00:19:25,654 --> 00:19:29,577
describe screen-based approximations and their
limitations.

258
00:19:29,577 --> 00:19:33,283
Actual comparisons need identified versions
and settings.

259
00:19:33,283 --> 00:19:37,983
A 2017 Unreal 4.16 demonstration approaches a
cube model and changes the AO

260
00:19:37,983 --> 00:19:42,982
Power and Bias controls. Watch the gaps
between cubes and their floor contacts.

261
00:19:42,982 --> 00:19:47,683
Adjusting this contact treatment is different
from adding a light source.

262
00:19:47,683 --> 00:19:51,975
Several controls and the camera change in this
sequence, so we will not attribute every

263
00:19:51,975 --> 00:19:56,444
difference to one isolated number. First
observe the actual manipulations and contacts;

264
00:19:56,444 --> 00:20:00,443
our separate diagram isolates sample range and
the limits of depth information.

265
00:20:00,443 --> 00:20:05,265
This is not a substitute for direct-light
shadows. Darkening corners does not calculate

266
00:20:05,265 --> 00:20:10,023
occlusion for a particular lamp or colored
indirect light reflected from another wall.

267
00:20:10,023 --> 00:20:13,952
Information outside the screen and behind
visible surfaces is still missing. The same

268
00:20:13,952 --> 00:20:18,150
information boundary becomes important for
screen-space reflections in the next episode.

269
00:20:18,870 --> 00:20:21,445
This episode followed assigned brightness,

270
00:20:21,445 --> 00:20:25,374
stored surface lighting, and updated
visibility for moving lights.

271
00:20:25,374 --> 00:20:29,303
More lights also changed the ordering of
material and lighting work.

272
00:20:29,303 --> 00:20:33,161
To distinguish methods, ask three questions:
what is stored,

273
00:20:33,161 --> 00:20:38,653
which changes require recomputation, and which
information is missing from the input?

274
00:20:38,653 --> 00:20:41,987
The next episode extends the discussion to
indirect light,

275
00:20:41,987 --> 00:20:46,122
reflections, water, and fog, connecting photon
mapping, SSR, Forward+,

276
00:20:46,122 --> 00:20:49,723
and clustered lighting to the different
problems they address.

277
00:20:49,723 --> 00:20:53,856
Reading techniques through their inputs and
processing order makes an engine

278
00:20:53,856 --> 00:20:57,676
frame easier to understand than names alone.
The programming-coaching

279
00:20:57,676 --> 00:21:00,870
link in the description is available for
hands-on learning.
